THE MATRIX WAS LOST TWICE BEFORE IT WAS READ. The first crossx run VOIDed with
"Disk quota exceeded (os error 122)" writing a cell's incremental build. /tmp on this machine
is a 16G RAM-BACKED tmpfs and crossx builds N^2 cargo target directories in it. The VOID guard
called it correctly -- "the matrix was lost to the harness, not to any candidate" -- and it
also explains the four pipeline kills attributed to low memory today: the build artefacts WERE
the memory. Scratch moved to disk; the re-run is this matrix.

THE SPEC CRITIQUE CONVERGED THE FIELD FOR THE THIRD WAVE RUNNING. All four arms named the same
three defects before implementing -- `next` required to be both unconditional and busy-filtered,
`Missing(NothingToMeasure)` written as a unit variant when `Absent::NothingToMeasure` carries a
required reason, and clause 7 demanding every `why` name a task or matrix that `QueueEmpty`
does not have -- and all four resolved them identically. None of the three split the field.

winner: glm-53-flash, and gemini-38-flash's critique argues it precisely: `own_name` returns
`None` for `QueueEmpty` so `Option<&str>::cmp` treats two of them as equal and the stable sort
preserves input order without dropping information, and both spec defects are handled "honestly
and correctly" rather than papered over. 18 tests in 582 lines against gemini's 32 in 970.

or-nemotron-ultra is out, on its own suite and on glm's critique of it, which is the sharpest
single piece of review in this wave:
  - `next_treats_missing_live_agents_as_busy` "cannot detect a clause-6 violation: with items
    [PipelineFailed, Adjudicate], busy and idle answers coincide, so a rival treating Missing
    agents as idle passes." The discriminating input is absent, and clause 6 is the safety
    clause this spec is loudest about. A test that looks like coverage and is none.
  - `priority_order_is_total_and_correct`'s `assert_eq!(types.len(), 5)` over a literal
    five-element array is trivially true.
  - its `why` for `QueueEmpty` hardcodes "no agents are running" while `next` hands that item
    out on the BUSY path.
Its suite fails all three rivals, which at N=4 is the N-1-of-N over-fitted shape.

THE THIRD FINDING IS ABOUT MY SPEC AND GLM SHARES IT. `QueueEmpty`'s doc says "Nothing is
queued and nothing is running", and clause 3 makes it `safe_while_busy: true`, so `next`
returns it while agents run. I defined the item as a claim about liveness and then handed it
out in the busy path. glm's own `why` restates the variant doc and inherits the same
contradiction. Recorded as a follow-up against the spec, not against the arm.

